@h1 Install & your first document h1@

Wit runs on Node 20+. Install the command-line tool, then build a real document
from an empty file — prose first, structure added one piece at a time.

@h2 Install the CLI h2@

@@@pre
npm install -g @witlang/cli
pre@@@

Check it landed:

@@@pre
wit --version
pre@@@

That prints @code 0.2.0 code@. The @code wit code@ command has no runtime
dependencies, so the install is quick. Below Node 20 the tool won't run — upgrade
Node, not Wit.

@h2 Write some prose h2@

Make a file called @code hike.wit code@ and type ordinary sentences. A blank line
starts a new paragraph; nothing else is required.

@@@pre
We reached Cairn Loop just after seven, ahead of the heat.

The ridge was quiet — a single hawk, and the wind doing all
the talking.
pre@@@

Build it to a web page:

@@@pre
wit build hike.wit -o hike.html
pre@@@

Open @code hike.html code@. Two clean paragraphs in a finished-looking theme — you
wrote no CSS, because the default HTML output is a complete, self-contained styled
page.

@h2 Add a heading and emphasis h2@

Put a title on top and mark two words. @code *stars* code@ make bold,
@code _underscores_ code@ make italic:

@@@pre
@h1 Weekend hike: Cairn Loop h1@

We reached Cairn Loop *just after seven*, ahead of the heat.
The ridge was _completely_ silent.
pre@@@

Rebuild and refresh. The @@@code @h1 … h1@ code@@@ is a real heading, the starred
words are bold, the underscored words italic. Everything you didn't mark stayed
exactly as typed — the marks wrap a single token, so @code 5*6*7 code@ and
@code file_name code@ are never touched.

@h2 Add a list h2@

@@@pre
@h2 What we packed h2@

@ul
  @li Two litres of water each li@
  @li Windshell and gloves li@
  @li Trail bars li@
ul@
pre@@@

That renders a @@@code <ul> code@@@ of three items. The indentation is for your
eyes only — a node's scope comes from its @@@code @ul … ul@ code@@@ pair, not from
columns. Swap @@@code @ul code@@@ for @@@code @ol code@@@ to number them.

@h2 Add a table h2@

Something Markdown can't do without dropping into HTML — a captioned table,
written inline:

@@@pre
@table |rows [[Segment, Distance, Time], [Trailhead to ridge, 4.2 km, 1h10], [Ridge to summit, 2.8 km, 55m]]| |caption Splits|
pre@@@

Wit builds a full @@@code <table> code@@@: a @@@code <caption> code@@@, a
@@@code <thead> code@@@ from the first row, and a @@@code <tbody> code@@@ from the
rest. The rows ride in a parameter because they're structured metadata.

@aside(class note) 💡 Cells in @@@code |rows [[…]]| code@@@ split on commas, and a
comma inside a cell can't be escaped — keep cell text comma-free, or build the
table from a @a |href /docs/guides/data/| data collection a@ instead. aside@

@h2 Add a figure h2@

A picture with a real caption (any @code .png code@ beside the file works for
@code src code@):

@@@pre
@figure
  @img |src ./ridge.png| |alt The ridge at dawn|
  @figcaption First light on the ridge. figcaption@
figure@
pre@@@

You get a semantic @@@code <figure> code@@@ wrapping the @@@code <img> code@@@ and
its @@@code <figcaption> code@@@ — the @code src code@ and @code alt code@ are
metadata in parameters; the caption is content in the body.

@h2 The whole file h2@

@@@pre
@h1 Weekend hike: Cairn Loop h1@

We reached Cairn Loop *just after seven*, ahead of the heat.
The ridge was _completely_ silent.

@h2 What we packed h2@

@ul
  @li Two litres of water each li@
  @li Windshell and gloves li@
  @li Trail bars li@
ul@

@table |rows [[Segment, Distance, Time], [Trailhead to ridge, 4.2 km, 1h10], [Ridge to summit, 2.8 km, 55m]]| |caption Splits|

@figure
  @img |src ./ridge.png| |alt The ridge at dawn|
  @figcaption First light on the ridge. figcaption@
figure@
pre@@@

One plain-text file — prose, a heading, emphasis, a list, a table, a figure — and
it built to HTML at every step.

@h2 The edit loop h2@

Editing Wit is three beats: @strong edit strong@ the file, run @strong build strong@,
@strong refresh strong@ the output. Two commands help alongside @code build code@:

@ul
@li @code wit check hike.wit code@ — report parse and reference errors without building. li@
@li @code wit fmt hike.wit code@ — tidy indentation without changing meaning (@code -w code@ writes it back). li@
ul@

@aside(class note) 💡 There's no @code --watch code@ flag yet. Builds are fast
enough to re-run on save, or wrap one in a loop:
@code while true; do wit build hike.wit -o hike.html; sleep 1; done code@. aside@

@h2 Build to PDF, Markdown, or a fragment h2@

The @code -o code@ extension picks the format:

@ul
@li @code -o hike.html code@ — a complete, styled, self-contained page. li@
@li @code -o hike.md code@ — clean Markdown, the same source for a different pipeline. li@
@li @code -o hike.pdf code@ — a paginated, print-ready PDF. li@
@li @strong No output flag strong@ — the HTML prints to your terminal, handy for piping. li@
ul@

Two flags fine-tune HTML: @code --fragment code@ emits just the bare
@@@code <article> code@@@ for pasting into an existing site, and @code --raw code@
swaps the built-in theme for a minimal reset so you can bring your own CSS.

PDF is the one format with a system requirement: Wit paginates its HTML through a
headless Chrome, so a system Chrome or Chromium must be installed. If none is
found, the build says so and names the fix:

@@@pre
wit build: no headless Chrome/Chromium found for PDF output.
Install Google Chrome, or point WIT_CHROME at a browser executable.
pre@@@

HTML and Markdown never need a browser; only PDF does.

